//
// `reduce` reads its accumulator type off the FOLD, not off the initial value (loft#956).
//
// A collection accumulator is refused rather than mis-compiled (loft#951's cut: the fold has
// to hand the callee a buffer per step, and a collection's is not the text one).  The refusal
// asked the INITIAL VALUE what type it was, and a bare `[]` has none to give — it arrives as
// `Unknown(0)`, which is not heap storage, so it walked straight past the refusal it was
// written for.  What came out the other side was a `reduce_acc` variable with no type:
// `--interpret` read it back EMPTY (`len=`, where `0` is the answer) and `--native` reached
// `rust_type` with a `Never` and panicked — *"Incorrect type Never"*, an internal compiler
// error with the user's line nowhere in it.  Both on released 2026.8.0 as well.
//
// The signature always knew.  `f: fn(U, T) -> U` names `U` in its first parameter and the
// init only has to be assignable to it, so the accumulator now comes from the fold and the
// literal only has to agree.  c1 and c2 are then the SAME program said two ways, and they get
// the same answer — which is the whole point: `[]` and `vector<integer>` were never different
// folds, only different amounts of writing-out.
//
// Two `vector<integer>` lines, one per cell: c1 and c2 are the same fold written two ways and
// the point is that they now answer IDENTICALLY, so both must appear.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot fold into a `vector<integer>` accumulator yet
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot fold into a `vector<integer>` accumulator yet
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot fold into a `vector<text>` accumulator yet
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot fold into a `index<Rec956[id]>` accumulator yet
//
// The keyed cell also pins the diagnostic's own spelling: `Type::name` used to interpolate a
// keyed collection's key list with `{:?}`, so a refusal read `index<Rec956,[("id", true)]>` —
// a Rust debug dump in a message a user is meant to act on. It now names the type the way the
// program writes it.

struct Rec956 { id: integer }

fn addv956(acc: vector<integer>, x: integer) -> vector<integer> { acc }
fn addt956(acc: vector<text>, x: text) -> vector<text> { acc }
fn addi956(acc: index<Rec956[id]>, x: integer) -> index<Rec956[id]> { acc }

fn main() {
  n = [1, 2, 3, 4];
  ts = ["a", "b"];

  // c1 — the filed shape: a bare `[]`.  Nothing here says what it is empty OF, so before the
  //      fix this was the one spelling that escaped the refusal.  It must now be refused with
  //      the type the FOLD names, not with silence.
  c1 = n.reduce([], addv956);

  // c2 (oracle) — the same fold with the accumulator written out.  This was always refused,
  //      and c1 must now answer identically: the diagnostic names `vector<integer>` in both.
  init: vector<integer> = [];
  c2 = n.reduce(init, addv956);

  // c3 — the element type is not the axis.  A `vector<text>` fold from a bare `[]` must name
  //      `vector<text>`, so the type is read off the fold rather than guessed from the input.
  c3 = ts.reduce([], addt956);

  // c4 — a KEYED collection, not just a vector.  `is_heap_storage` covers every collection
  //      kind, and a bare `[]` is how all of them are spelled empty, so the hole was the same
  //      width for each.
  c4 = n.reduce([], addi956);

  // Every fold above is refused, so nothing here runs — the cells are the diagnostics. The
  // uses keep each result live so no dead-code pass can drop the call before it is checked.
  println("{len(c1)}{len(c2)}{len(c3)}{len(c4)}");
}
